## Solution

We are given
$$f(x) = \int_{1/x}^{x} e^{\,t+1/t}\,\frac{dt}{t}, \qquad \text{and asked to evaluate } \int_{-1}^{1} f(2^x)\,dx.$$

**Key observation: antisymmetry of $f$ under $x \mapsto 1/x$.**

Compute $f(1/x)$:
$$f(1/x) = \int_{1/(1/x)}^{1/x} e^{t+1/t}\,\frac{dt}{t} = \int_{x}^{1/x} e^{t+1/t}\,\frac{dt}{t} = -\int_{1/x}^{x} e^{t+1/t}\,\frac{dt}{t} = -f(x).$$

So
$$f(1/x) = -f(x). \tag{1}$$

(As a check, this also gives $f(1)=0$, consistent with the limits of integration coinciding.)

**Apply to the integrand.** Let $g(x) = f(2^x)$. Then
$$g(-x) = f(2^{-x}) = f\!\left(\tfrac{1}{2^x}\right) \stackrel{(1)}{=} -f(2^x) = -g(x).$$

Hence $g$ is an **odd function** on $\mathbb{R}$.

**Conclude.** The interval $[-1,1]$ is symmetric about $0$, and the integral of an odd function over a symmetric interval is zero:
$$\int_{-1}^{1} f(2^x)\,dx = \int_{-1}^{1} g(x)\,dx = 0.$$

The correct option is **(c) 0**.

